  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 497c1470319f9304a83a02020aaa9e30, type: 3}
  m_Name: DodoStory
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::ExhibitStory
  animalName: The Dodo
  scientificName: Raphus cucullatus
  status: EXTINCT
  pages:
  - body: The Dodo was a flightless bird endemic to the island of Mauritius in the Indian Ocean. Standing about 1 metre tall and weighing up to 18 kilograms, it lived peacefully in forests, nesting on the ground and feeding on fallen fruits. It had no natural predators and no fear of other animals.
  - body: When Dutch sailors arrived in 1598, the dodo had no natural fear of humans. Sailors hunted them freely for food, and introduced species such as rats, pigs, and monkeys destroyed dodo ground nests and ate their eggs, eliminating entire breeding populations within years.
  - body: The extinction of the Dodo by 1681 is the archetypal example of human-induced extinction. It teaches us the extreme vulnerability of island ecosystems and the devastating speed at which invasive species and human activity can erase a species from existence forever.
